<!--
  The two declarations that make Headway a car-app host, and the two that can
  stop it installing.

  They are in a flavour of their own because they are the only things in the
  whole manifest whose cost is paid by the entire app. A permission name has
  exactly one definer per device; a provider authority exactly one owner. On a
  phone where something else already holds either — realistically, one with
  Google's Android Auto — an APK that declares them fails to install outright,
  and the platform's own report for that is the bare string "App not
  installed". Every unrelated feature goes down with it.

  So: the `host` flavour claims them and gets the car-app host; the `compat`
  flavour claims nothing and installs anywhere. Same applicationId, same key,
  so either upgrades the other with no uninstall. See app/build.gradle.kts,
  docs/adr/0007-headway-as-a-car-app-host.md, and BLOCKERS B-013 / B-014.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      The car-app template host, declared and then held by Headway itself.

      This is the single line that decides whether a third-party car app will
      talk to Headway at all, and it is worth stating exactly why it works.

      An app that offers a car UI does so through a CarAppService, and before it
      answers anything it runs HostValidator.isValidHost() in its own process.
      That method accepts a caller on four conditions and no others
      (HostValidator.validateHost, decompiled from androidx.car.app:app:1.7.0):
      the caller is the app itself; the caller's package and signing digest are
      in the allowlist the app hardcoded; the caller is uid 1000; or the caller
      holds android.car.permission.TEMPLATE_RENDERER, checked as
      REQUESTED_PERMISSION_GRANTED on the caller's own PackageInfo.

      Headway cannot be any of the first three. It can be the fourth, because a
      permission is just a name: an app may declare one and use it, and a
      signature-protected permission is granted at install to the package that
      declared it, itself. No root, no system signature, no adb, no
      privileged API. The permission names a role, and Headway genuinely
      performs that role: it renders car-app templates on a car screen.

      This is not impersonation and cannot become it. Headway still tells every
      app its real package name, and the library cross-checks that name against
      the real calling uid: HostValidator.validateHost looks the claimed package
      up and throws IllegalStateException when its uid does not match the
      HostInfo it was handed. CarAppBinder.onHandshakeCompleted supplies that
      HostInfo from Binder.getCallingUid() and does no checking of its own, so
      the guarantee lives one frame further in than an earlier version of this
      comment said. It is reached on the permission branch, which is the branch
      Headway takes. An app that would rather not talk to Headway can still
      refuse it by name.

      signature rather than normal, so nothing else on the phone can pick the
      permission up from Headway's declaration.

      The one real cost: a package that already declares this permission makes
      Headway fail to install with INSTALL_FAILED_DUPLICATE_PERMISSION. Nothing
      on an AOSP phone declares it: it belongs to Android Automotive's car
      service, which is not present. Google's Android Auto app might, so a
      phone running that alongside Headway is the case to watch. BLOCKERS B-013
      records it with the workaround.
    -->
    <permission
        android:name="android.car.permission.TEMPLATE_RENDERER"
        android:protectionLevel="signature" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.car.permission.TEMPLATE_RENDERER" />

    <application>
        <!--
          The car-connection provider every car app queries to find out whether
          it is being projected.

          androidx.car.app.connection.CarConnection does not ask the system; it
          queries this well-known authority and reads one integer. On a phone
          with Android Auto installed, Gearhead answers it. On a de-Googled
          phone nothing does, and every car app concludes it is not in a car,
          which for several of them means refusing to run their car service at
          all. Headway answers with the truth about its own AAP session.

          Exported because the whole point is that other apps read it, and there
          is nothing to protect: the answer is whether a car is connected, which
          any app can already see from Bluetooth. Read-only; every write path in
          CarConnectionProvider is refused.

          The authority is exclusive, so a phone that already has Android Auto
          installed will refuse to install Headway with
          INSTALL_FAILED_CONFLICTING_PROVIDER. BLOCKERS B-014 records it and the
          way out; templates render either way.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name=".carapp.CarConnectionProvider"
            android:authorities="androidx.car.app.connection"
            android:exported="true"
            tools:ignore="ExportedContentProvider" />
    </application>
</manifest>
